  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 304 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe main concern both today and tomorrow will be for storms with \nvery heavy rainfall and a local flash flood threat. A couple \nsevere storms with damaging winds will also be possible. The \ngreatest threat of flash flooding and severe weather will be east\nof the I-25 Corridor. \n\nHere's the setup. An easterly push of higher dewpoint air was\npushing back toward the I-25 Corridor in the wee morning hours,\na result of outflow from overnight storms in Nebraska and Kansas.\nDewpoints were in the lower to mid 60s over the northeast plains\nbehind that surge. That surge is expected to wash out along the\nI-25 Corridor in the pre-dawn hours, but will be enhancing the low\nlevel moisture and instability across a good chunk of the\nnortheast plains today. Unlike yesterday when the mesoscale\nconvective vortex (MCV) interrupted normal convective development\nand evolution, today should be a little more straight-forward.\nBarring the mid/high convective cloud remnants over the \nsouth/eastern portions of our forecast area (at least early in the\nday) we should see sufficient cloud thinning for boundary later \nheating and destabilization. Forecast soundings also show a weaker\ninversion, so we should be able to reach convective temps in all\nareas. Storms will first fire over the mountains and foothills by\nearly afternoon, and then spread eastward across the plains in the\nmean westerly flow aloft on the northern periphery of the ridge.\n\nRegarding the heavy rain and flash flood threat, precipitable \nwater values are still forecast to range from 150-200% of normal \nacross the forecast area, reaching daily climatological maximums \n(near 1.3\" for Denver and 1.5+\" for the eastern plains. Warm cloud\ndepths will also increase to ~5,000 feet driving rainfall \nefficiency. Instability will also be greater, supporting stronger\nand larger storms. The one limiting factor is expected storm \nmotion near 20 mph as the storms organize onto the eastern plains.\nWhile the foothills and 2020/2024 burn scars will see locally \nheavy rain, the storm size and motions should be sufficient to \nkeep most precipitation amounts below critical thresholds. That \ncould very well change as storms intensify as they head east of \nI-25. That's where they will encounter very rich dewpoints in the \nlower to mid 60s and stronger instability with MLCAPE of 1500-2000\nJ/kg and even deeper warm cloud depths. Thus, I would expect \nthese storms to be very efficient rain producers with rain rates \nof 2\" or more in just 30-45 minutes. Local Probability Matched \nMeans (LPMM) suggest isolated 3-4\" amounts in a relatively narrow \ncorridor from northern Weld/Logan Counties southward through \nMorgan, Washington, and eastern Elbert and Lincoln Counties. Given\nthe ingredients, we've issued a Flood Watch for flash flooding \nfor those particular areas where the threat is greatest. \n\nOne other thing to note, there is a slight chance that once\nstronger storms organize over the plains a resultant outflow \nboundary backs into the foothills generating a second round of \nstorms for the evening. Thus, that's something to watch for the \nfoothills and I-25 Corridor if we don't have widespread convection\nthis afternoon that takes all the instability with it. Those \nlatter storms would be capable of producing heavier rainfall so \nsomething to monitor through this evening. \n\nGiven the MLCAPE magnitude and profiles, the main severe weather\nthreat would be from organized linear structures and wet\nmicrobursts. There would be some hail threat as well (would\nnormally be quite large given the MLCAPE, except today we'll have\nconsiderable melting of hailstones on descent in this warm/wet \nenvironment). \n\nFor Wednesday, it looks like a rinse/repeat scenario for the most\npart. This time, the deeper moisture and instability has a greater\nchance to spread back to the I-25 Corridor so there would be\npotential for stronger storms and heavy rainfall farther west.\nThat said, both days the more organized storms are expected east\nof the I-25 Corridor so not all areas are guaranteed to receive  \nbeneficial/soaking rainfall.\n\nWe don't see much change in the ensemble forecasts for the latter\npart of the week, showing gradual drying and lowering PW Thursday\nthrough Saturday. We'll transition into northwest flow aloft as\nthe upper level ridge retrogrades into the Desert Southwest. So\nthat should keep the hottest temperatures away until sometime this\nweekend into early next week. It still looks like Sunday will be\nheating up to near 100 degrees across the plains and I-25\nCorridor, potentially lasting into Monday depending on the speed\nof a shortwave moving across the U.S./Canadian border.",
